# KNOWN GAP — SELF-HOST LANE ONLY. A `@derive(Hashable, Equatable)` key/element
# type used in a Dict or Set does not survive the self-host lowerer's dead-code
# elimination: the generated hasher's own callees are never marked reachable, so
# the emitted C fails to LINK.
#
#   undefined reference to `FxHasher__write_int'
#   undefined reference to `FxHasher__write_string'
#   undefined reference to `hash_of__Named'
#
# Rust gg compiles, links and runs this correctly on both backends — this is a
# LANE DIVERGENCE, not a language question.
#
# AXIS CELL: self-host lane × `@derive(Hashable, Equatable)` struct × used as a
# Dict key AND as a Set element. The DCE root seeding reaches `T__hash` / `T__eq`
# themselves (`lir_codegen.gg`, the `collect_hashable_key_types` seeding loop) but
# the transitive walk does not reach the `FxHasher` methods those hashers call,
# nor the `hash_of__T` glue.
#
# ⚠ PRE-EXISTING, and measured as such rather than assumed: reproduced against
# the UNMODIFIED self-host driver, before the typed set allocator landed. Same
# family as the corpus's `method_generic_trait_dispatch` CC-FAIL
# (`undefined reference to Point__custom_hash__FxHasher`).
#
# ⚠ Also affects the live fixtures `dict_user_key_hashable.gg` and
# `set_user_key_hashable.gg` on the self-host lane.
#
# INTENDED: links on the self-host lane and prints `1` then `true`, matching
# Rust gg.
#
# Wired test: known_gap_sh_derive_hashable_key_dce (tests/integration.rs,
# #[ignore]d).

@derive(Hashable, Equatable)
struct Named:
    String name
    int n

void main():
    Dict[Named, int] d = Dict[Named, int]()
    d.set(Named("a", 1), 7)
    print(d.len())
    Set[Named] s = Set[Named]()
    s.add(Named("a", 1))
    print(s.contains(Named("a", 1)))
